  "theoretical_insights": {
    "why_rigorous_bounds_are_conservative": "The operator norm ||A^-1||_inf scales as O(N) = O(1/h^2). At 256x256, ||A^-1||_inf is ~15,000 to 25,000 K/W. Because ||e||_inf <= ||A^-1||_inf * ||r||_inf assumes all cell residuals reinforce each other with uniform sign, it ignores the high-frequency oscillatory nature of neural operator errors. As a result, the bound is loose by a factor of 1,000x to 5,000x, rejecting 100% of surrogate predictions under any practical MaxAE <= 10 K threshold.",
    "exact_barrier_majorant_computational_paradox": "The exact elementwise monotone upper bound |e| <= A^-1 |r| requires solving a full sparse linear system A w = |r|. Because A is identical in size, sparsity, and condition number to the original FVM discretization, solving A w = |r| costs exactly 100% of the direct FVM solve time. Computing a certified bound via exact inversion destroys the entire surrogate acceleration advantage (0.5x speedup).",
    "preconditioned_jacobi_as_practical_estimator": "The pointwise Jacobi residual ||D^-1 r||_inf is physically calibrated in Kelvin and computable in O(N) operations (0.05 ms at 256x256). It achieves high rank correlation (rho ~ 0.80) with actual MaxAE and AUROC > 0.85 for 10 K violations. However, because it neglects long-wavelength error smoothing, it is a numerical estimator with a non-zero underestimation rate (~10-15%), requiring empirical calibration safety factors rather than rigorous mathematical certification."
  }
